ug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="172">It looks like youâ€™re gathering sources on <strong data-start="42" data-end="71">postclassical narratology</strong>. Hereâ€™s a structured overview of the key references you listed, with context on their contributions:</p>
<hr data-start="174" data-end="177">
<h2 data-start="179" data-end="191">Core Book</h2>
<ul data-start="192" data-end="886">
<li data-start="192" data-end="886">
<p data-start="194" data-end="886"><strong data-start="194" data-end="324">Jan Alber &amp; Monika Fludernik (eds.), <em data-start="233" data-end="285">Postclassical Narratology: Approaches and Analyses</em> (Ohio State University Press, 2010).</strong><br data-start="324" data-end="327">
This is the foundational edited volume that maps out the field. It collects essays extending classical narratology (structuralist narratology of Genette, Barthes, Todorov, etc.) into new interdisciplinary domains: cognitive narratology, feminist narratology, unnatural narratology, rhetorical narratology, transmedial narratology, and more. It emphasizes how â€œpostclassicalâ€ should not be conflated with â€œpoststructuralistâ€â€”rather, it preserves classical narratology as one â€œmomentâ€ and develops outward from it. Widely cited as the definitive introduction.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="888" data-end="891">
<h2 data-start="893" data-end="918">Articles and Overviews</h2>
<ul data-start="919" data-end="1931">
<li data-start="919" data-end="1124">
<p data-start="921" data-end="1124"><strong data-start="921" data-end="974">ResearchGate summary of Alber &amp; Fludernik (2010).</strong><br data-start="974" data-end="977">
Reviews the book as a timely overview of the field, noting its interdisciplinarity and the variety of methods now under the narratology umbrella.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1126" data-end="1420">
<p data-start="1128" data-end="1420"><strong data-start="1128" data-end="1167">Ruth Sommer (via DNB, cited by 57).</strong><br data-start="1167" data-end="1170">
Discusses postclassical narratologyâ€™s â€œsecond stageâ€ of development, describing the fieldâ€™s maturation from adolescence into consolidation. Suggests that while there is diversity, there are now shared assumptions and clearer methodological anchors.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1422" data-end="1663">
<p data-start="1424" data-end="1663"><strong data-start="1424" data-end="1477">Oxford Academic â€“ â€œPostclassicism | Narratology.â€</strong><br data-start="1477" data-end="1480">
Explores how postclassical narratology integrates the role of the reader, real-world experiences, and reception into narrative theory. Stresses â€œco-poieticâ€ aspects of storytelling.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1665" data-end="1931">
<p data-start="1667" data-end="1931"><strong data-start="1667" data-end="1735">Ionescu (2019), â€œPostclassical Narratology: Twenty Years Later.â€</strong><br data-start="1735" data-end="1738">
Retrospective tracing the origins further back to the 1940s (pre-structuralist foreshadowings). Situates the field historically and evaluates its trajectory after two decades of theorization.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1933" data-end="1936">
<h2 data-start="1938" data-end="1979">Abstracts and Institutional References</h2>
<ul data-start="1980" data-end="2458">
<li data-start="1980" data-end="2264">
<p data-start="1982" data-end="2264"><strong data-start="1982" data-end="2076">Stockholm University abstract (â€œThe Merger of Classical and Postclassical Narratologiesâ€).</strong><br data-start="2076" data-end="2079">
Frames the relationship between the structuralist â€œclassicalâ€ phase and the pluralistic â€œpostclassicalâ€ one, suggesting they are not mutually exclusive but coexistent â€œnarratologies.â€</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2266" data-end="2458">
<p data-start="2268" data-end="2458"><strong data-start="2268" data-end="2316">DNB (German National Library) catalog entry.</strong><br data-start="2316" data-end="2319">
Confirms the fieldâ€™s reception in European contexts, marking the transition from early enthusiasm to a more institutionalized discipline.</p>
