<h2 data-start="2755" data-end="2771">Key Takeaways</h2>
<ol data-start="2772" data-end="3402">
<li data-start="2772" data-end="2926">
<p data-start="2775" data-end="2926"><strong data-start="2775" data-end="2789">Definition</strong>: Postclassical narratology = narratology after structuralism, not against it. It expands into interdisciplinary and empirical domains.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2927" data-end="3056">
<p data-start="2930" data-end="3056"><strong data-start="2930" data-end="2939">Scope</strong>: Cognitive science, media studies, feminist theory, rhetorical analysis, unnatural narratives, digital narratives.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3057" data-end="3227">
<p data-start="3060" data-end="3227"><strong data-start="3060" data-end="3085">Historical trajectory</strong>: From structuralist models (1960sâ€“70s), through a pluralistic expansion (1990sâ€“2000s), into a mature interdisciplinary field (2010sâ€“2020s).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3228" data-end="3402">
<p data-start="3231" data-end="3402"><strong data-start="3231" data-end="3250">Current debates</strong>: The balance between methodological rigor (structuralist inheritance) and theoretical openness (reader-response, cultural studies, digital humanities).</p>
</li>
