<p data-start="293" data-end="339">Let me restitch the examples more carefully:</p>
<hr data-start="341" data-end="344">
<h2 data-start="346" data-end="383">The Mask of Survival Across Eras</h2>
<p data-start="385" data-end="537">The paradox of image is recurrent in history and literature: to survive, one must manipulate appearances, often by <strong data-start="500" data-end="534">lowering or disguising oneself</strong>.</p>
<ul data-start="539" data-end="1612">
<li data-start="539" data-end="795">
<p data-start="541" data-end="795"><strong data-start="541" data-end="576">King David (1 Samuel 21:10â€“15).</strong> Pursued by Saul, David feigns madness before King Achish of Gath, scribbling on doors and letting spittle run down his beard. His strategy is degradation: to escape suspicion by appearing unworthy of fear or respect.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="797" data-end="1027">
<p data-start="799" data-end="1027"><strong data-start="799" data-end="833">Giordano Bruno (16th century).</strong> The philosopherâ€™s cosmological heresies forced him into perpetual disguise, sometimes even posing as a villager. To be recognized as himself was fatal; only anonymity could delay persecution.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1029" data-end="1323">
<p data-start="1031" data-end="1323"><strong data-start="1031" data-end="1099">Rumata of Estor (<em data-start="1050" data-end="1068">Hard to Be a God</em>, Strugatsky brothers, 1964).</strong> An Earth-born observer stationed on a planet stuck in feudal stagnation, Rumata must conceal his knowledge and advanced identity. His â€œnoblemanâ€ guise keeps him alive, but his true role and convictions must remain hidden.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1325" data-end="1612">
<p data-start="1327" data-end="1612"><strong data-start="1327" data-end="1395">Maxim Kammerer (<em data-start="1345" data-end="1364">Prisoner of Power</em>, Strugatsky brothers, 1969).</strong> Though not in <em data-start="1410" data-end="1428">Hard to Be a God</em>, Maxim embodies the same Noon Universe theme: the Earth-born protagonist trapped in oppressive societies, forced to navigate between concealment and revelation of his real identity.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1614" data-end="1617">
<h2 data-start="1619" data-end="1659">The Continuity of the Image Problem</h2>
<p data-start="1661" data-end="1716">These cases illustrate a <strong data-start="1686" data-end="1713">structural law of image</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="1717" data-end="2105">
<li data-start="1717" data-end="1763">
<p data-start="1719" data-end="1763"><strong data-start="1719" data-end="1728">David</strong> survives by degrading his image.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1764" data-end="1836">
<p data-start="1766" data-end="1836"><strong data-start="1766" data-end="1775">Bruno</strong> survives (temporarily) by effacing his intellectual image.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1837" data-end="1934">
<p data-start="1839" data-end="1934"><strong data-start="1839" data-end="1849">Rumata</strong> survives by donning an aristocratic disguise while suppressing his true knowledge.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1935" data-end="2105">
<p data-start="1937" data-end="2105"><strong data-start="1937" data-end="1946">Maxim</strong> (in your own work, <em data-start="1966" data-end="1984">Call from Ankyra</em>, and in <em data-start="1993" data-end="2012">Prisoner of Power</em>) is another node in this same dialectic: the protagonist trapped by regimes of appearance.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2107" data-end="2421">What unites them is not the details of their disguises, but the fact that <strong data-start="2181" data-end="2236">truth cannot be lived openly under regimes of image</strong>. Whether the danger is persecution (David, Bruno) or irrelevance/hypervisibility (contemporary self-promotion), the mechanism is the same: image governs survival more than substance.</p>
<hr data-start="2423" data-end="2426">
<p data-start="2428" data-end="2722" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to <strong data-start="2449" data-end="2506">integrate this correction into the larger essay draft</strong> so that Bruno, David, Rumata, and Maxim form a <em data-start="2554" data-end="2569">constellation</em>? That way the historical, biblical, philosophical, and science-fictional examples line up as a single argument about the tyranny of image across epochs.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.2968